  data: {
    hero: {
      title: 'Teen Do Panch (3-2-5)',
      subtitle: 'Trick-taking',
    },
    sections: [
      {
        type: 'about',
        tabLabel: 'About',
        pages: [
          {
            title: 'Teen Do Panch (3-2-5)',
            subtitle: 'Card Game',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "3-2-5 (Teen Do Panch, also 2-3-5 or 5-3-2) is a trick-taking card game played in India, Pakistan, and Nepal. Three players use a 30-card deck. Each has a trick quota: dealer 2, player to dealer's right 5, player to dealer's left 3. The 5-trick player chooses trump and leads. Players above quota can 'pull' cards from those below in the next deal.",
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'South Asia (India, Pakistan, Nepal). Closely related to Sergeant Major (3-5-8 / 8-5-3) played with 52 cards.',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Setup',
            subtitle: 'Table, deck, equipment, and deal',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "Exactly 3. Dealer has quota 2 tricks; player to dealer's right 5 tricks (chooses trump, leads); player to dealer's left 3 tricks.",
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '30-card pack: remove 2s through 6s from 52, plus remove two 7s (usually keep 7♥ and 7♠). Rank A-K-Q-J-10-9-8-7 high to low.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '30-card deck.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Three batches: 5 cards, then 3, then 2 to each player (10 each). Deal and play usually counter-clockwise.',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Variations',
            subtitle: 'Known ways this game changes',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'list',
                style: 'unordered',
                items: [
                  {
                    text: "Clockwise Deal and Play: Deal and play proceed clockwise instead of counter-clockwise, with the player to dealer's left taking the 5-trick quota, choosing trumps, and leading to the first trick while the player to dealer's right has the 3-trick quota.",
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Seventh Card Turned for Trump: Instead of freely announcing a trump suit, the 5-trick player may specify that the seventh card dealt to them (the middle card of the second batch of three) will be turned up and its suit becomes trumps.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Next Batch Highest Card Trump: Some play that the 5-trick player may choose trumps to be the suit of the highest card in the next batch of three cards dealt to them, or a randomly chosen card from the remainder of their hand.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Relaxed Return Suit Requirement: Instead of needing at least two remaining cards in the suit being returned after pulling, some play that a player who steals a card need only keep one card in that suit when choosing which card to give back.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Sevens as Permanent Trumps: In some circles both Sevens are permanent trumps: the 7 of hearts is the highest trump, followed by the 7 of spades, then the remaining cards of the chosen trump suit in their usual order.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Bonus for Winning All Ten Tricks: Some players award an extra 5 points, for a total of 15, to any player who succeeds in winning all ten tricks in a deal.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Trick Transfer and Loser Deals: Variants allow a below-quota player to transfer a trick to an above-quota player instead of giving up a card and may also make the biggest loser of a deal the next dealer instead of rotating the deal normally.',
                  },
                ],
              },
            ],
          },
        ],
      },
    ],
